\details{
Once the peak list has been read using the `readrtdata` function and
the corresponding `rtdata` object has been generated, the peak list can be
filtered and new annotations can be predicted. To retain only high-confidence
annotations that follow the expected elution pattern, the filterannotations
function is applied. This function integrates a set of internal routines designed
to remove lipid annotations that do not comply with the expected intra-class
and inter-class elution order, based on a series of predefined rules and criteria.
To allow flexibility and user control, several parameters can be adjusted,
enabling filtering to be tailored to specific analytical requirements.

The filtering procedure is performed sequentially. First, for features
associated with multiple annotations, those with lower confidence levels are
discarded, retaining only the most reliable annotation. Second, in-source fragments
corresponding to the MG, DG, LPL, and FA subclasses are removed. Third, outliers
within each lipid class are identified and eliminated based on the number of
carbon atoms, removing compounds that deviate markedly from their homologous
series and from the overall behavior of the class. The fourth step aims to
improve plasmalogen annotation. To this end, a priority order among subsubclasses
is defined (diacyl > alkenyl > alkanyl), such that when lipids with identical
compositions are detected, annotations are retained according to this hierarchy
and their consistency with the expected RT order, while lower-priority annotations
are removed. Next, lipids that do not follow the expected RT elution order within
